#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:9
msgid "A step towards the Single Particle Model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:20
msgid ""
"In the `previous notebook <./2-a-pde-model.ipynb>`__ we saw how to solve a "
"PDE model in pybamm. Now it is time to solve a real-life battery problem! We "
"consider the problem of spherical diffusion in the negative electrode "
"particle within the single particle model. That is,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:22
msgid "\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial t} = \\nabla \\cdot (D \\nabla c),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:27
msgid "with the following boundary and initial conditions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:29
msgid ""
"\\left.\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial r}\\right\\vert_{r=0} = 0, \\quad "
"\\left.\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial r}\\right\\vert_{r=R} = -\\frac{j}{FD}, "
"\\quad \\left.c\\right\\vert_{t=0} = c_0,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:34
msgid ""
"where :math:`c` is the concentration, :math:`r` the radial coordinate, :math:"
"`t` time, :math:`R` the particle radius, :math:`D` the diffusion "
"coefficient, :math:`j` the interfacial current density, :math:`F` Faraday's "
"constant, and :math:`c_0` the initial concentration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:36
msgid "In this example we use the following parameters:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:39
msgid "Symbol"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:39
msgid "Units"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:39
msgid "Value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:41
msgid ":math:`R`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:41
msgid "m"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:41
msgid ":math:`10 \\times 10^{-6}`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:43
msgid ":math:`D`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:43
msgid "m\\ :math:`{^2}` s\\ :math:`^{-1}`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:43
msgid ":math:`3.9 \\times 10^{-14}`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:45
msgid ":math:`j`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:45
msgid "A m\\ :math:`^{-2}`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:45
msgid ":math:`1.4`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:47
msgid ":math:`F`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:47
msgid "C mol\\ :math:`^{-1}`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:47
msgid ":math:`96485`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:49
msgid ":math:`c_0`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:49
msgid "mol m\\ :math:`^{-3}`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:49
msgid ":math:`2.5 \\times 10^{4}`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:62
msgid "Setting up the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:64
msgid ""
"As before, we begin by importing the PyBaMM library into this notebook, "
"along with any other packages we require, and start with an empty ``pybamm."
"BaseModel``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid "[1]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"%pip install \"pybamm[plot,cite]\" -q    # install PyBaMM if it is not "
"installed\n"
"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n"
"\n"
"import pybamm\n"
"\n"
"model = pybamm.BaseModel()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:90
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:96
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:103
msgid "Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:114
msgid ""
"We then define all of the model variables and parameters. Parameters are "
"created using the ``pybamm.Parameter`` class and are given informative names "
"(with units). Later, we will provide parameter values and the ``Parameter`` "
"objects will be turned into numerical values. For more information please "
"see the `parameter values notebook <../parameterization/parameter-values."
"ipynb>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid "[2]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"R = pybamm.Parameter(\"Particle radius [m]\")\n"
"D = pybamm.Parameter(\"Diffusion coefficient [m2.s-1]\")\n"
"j = pybamm.Parameter(\"Interfacial current density [A.m-2]\")\n"
"F = pybamm.Parameter(\"Faraday constant [C.mol-1]\")\n"
"c0 = pybamm.Parameter(\"Initial concentration [mol.m-3]\")\n"
"\n"
"c = pybamm.Variable(\"Concentration [mol.m-3]\", domain=\"negative "
"particle\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:141
msgid ""
"Now we define our model equations, boundary and initial conditions, as in "
"the previous example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid "[3]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# governing equations\n"
"N = -D * pybamm.grad(c)  # flux\n"
"dcdt = -pybamm.div(N)\n"
"model.rhs = {c: dcdt}\n"
"\n"
"# boundary conditions\n"
"lbc = pybamm.Scalar(0)\n"
"rbc = -j / F / D\n"
"model.boundary_conditions = {c: {\"left\": (lbc, \"Neumann\"), \"right\": "
"(rbc, \"Neumann\")}}\n"
"\n"
"# initial conditions\n"
"model.initial_conditions = {c: c0}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:173
msgid ""
"Finally, we add any variables of interest to the dictionary ``model."
"variables``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid "[4]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"model.variables = {\n"
"    \"Concentration [mol.m-3]\": c,\n"
"    \"Surface concentration [mol.m-3]\": pybamm.surf(c),\n"
"    \"Flux [mol.m-2.s-1]\": N,\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:199
msgid "Using the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:210
msgid ""
"In order to discretise and solve the model we need to provide values for all "
"of the parameters. This is done via the ``pybamm.ParameterValues`` class, "
"which accepts a dictionary of parameter names and values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid "[5]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"param = pybamm.ParameterValues(\n"
"    {\n"
"        \"Particle radius [m]\": 10e-6,\n"
"        \"Diffusion coefficient [m2.s-1]\": 3.9e-14,\n"
"        \"Interfacial current density [A.m-2]\": 1.4,\n"
"        \"Faraday constant [C.mol-1]\": 96485,\n"
"        \"Initial concentration [mol.m-3]\": 2.5e4,\n"
"    }\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:239
msgid ""
"Here all of the parameters are simply scalars, but they can also be "
"functions or read in from data (see `parameter values notebook <../"
"parameterization/parameter-values.ipynb>`__)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:250
msgid ""
"As in the previous example, we define the particle geometry. Note that in "
"this example the definition of the geometry contains a parameter, the "
"particle radius :math:`R`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid "[6]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"r = pybamm.SpatialVariable(\n"
"    \"r\", domain=[\"negative particle\"], coord_sys=\"spherical polar\"\n"
")\n"
"geometry = {\"negative particle\": {r: {\"min\": pybamm.Scalar(0), \"max\": "
"R}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:274
msgid ""
"Both the model and geometry can now be processed by the parameter class. "
"This replaces the parameters with the values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid "[7]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"param.process_model(model)\n"
"param.process_geometry(geometry)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:296
msgid ""
"We can now set up our mesh, choose a spatial method, and discretise our model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid "[8]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"submesh_types = {\"negative particle\": pybamm.Uniform1DSubMesh}\n"
"var_pts = {r: 20}\n"
"mesh = pybamm.Mesh(geometry, submesh_types, var_pts)\n"
"\n"
"spatial_methods = {\"negative particle\": pybamm.FiniteVolume()}\n"
"disc = pybamm.Discretisation(mesh, spatial_methods)\n"
"disc.process_model(model);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:323
msgid "The model is now discretised and ready to be solved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:335
msgid "Solving the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:346
msgid ""
"As is the previous example, we choose a solver and times at which we want "
"the solution returned."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid "[9]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# solve\n"
"solver = pybamm.IDAKLUSolver()\n"
"t = [0, 3600]\n"
"solution = solver.solve(model, t)\n"
"\n"
"# post-process, so that the solution can be called at any time t or space r\n"
"# (using interpolation)\n"
"c = solution[\"Concentration [mol.m-3]\"]\n"
"c_surf = solution[\"Surface concentration [mol.m-3]\"]\n"
"\n"
"# plot\n"
"fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(13, 4))\n"
"\n"
"ax1.plot(solution.t, c_surf(solution.t))\n"
"ax1.set_xlabel(\"Time [s]\")\n"
"ax1.set_ylabel(\"Surface concentration [mol.m-3]\")\n"
"\n"
"r = mesh[\"negative particle\"].nodes  # radial position\n"
"time = 1000  # time in seconds\n"
"ax2.plot(r * 1e6, c(t=time, r=r), label=f\"t={time}[s]\")\n"
"ax2.set_xlabel(\"Particle radius [microns]\")\n"
"ax2.set_ylabel(\"Concentration [mol.m-3]\")\n"
"ax2.legend()\n"
"\n"
"plt.tight_layout()\n"
"plt.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb:400
msgid ""
"In the `next notebook <./4-comparing-full-and-reduced-order-models.ipynb>`__ "
"we consider the limit of fast diffusion in the particle. This leads to a "
"reduced-order model for the particle behaviour, which we compare with the "
"full (Fickian diffusion) model."
msgstr ""

